got you — frame-by-frame animation in React comes down to: preload your images, then swap which one's showing on a timer. *tail wag*

1. make a component, and track the current frame index with `useState`.
2. define an array of image URLs, one per frame.
3. use `useEffect` to preload all the images into the browser's cache — keeps the animation from stuttering while it waits on image loads.
4. use `setInterval` (or `setTimeout`) to advance the frame index on a regular beat — the interval speed is your frame rate knob.
5. render the current frame's image, usually with a plain `img` tag.
6. import the component into your page and drop it into your JSX.

here's the whole thing put together:

```javascript
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const Animation = () => {
  const [currentFrame, setCurrentFrame] = useState(0);
  const frames = [
    'frame1.png',
    'frame2.png',
    'frame3.png',
    'frame4.png',
    // add more frames as needed
  ];

  useEffect(() => {
    // preload all the images
    frames.forEach((frame) => {
      new Image().src = frame;
    });

    // update the frame index every 100ms
    const interval = setInterval(() => {
      setCurrentFrame((frame) => (frame + 1) % frames.length);
    }, 100);

    return () => clearInterval(interval);
  }, []);

  return (
    <img src={frames[currentFrame]} alt="frame" />
  );
};

export default Animation;
```

and to drop it on your page:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import Animation from './Animation';

const App = () => {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>My Animation</h1>
      <Animation />
    </div>
  );
};

export default App;
```

